This study explores the visual representation of visualizing tools designed for three-dimensional generalized Hénon maps, noting that while such tools have been extensively developed for two-dimensional cases, their application to three-dimensional generalized Hénon maps remains underdeveloped. The primary objective is to assess and compare various visualization methods to identify a suitable approach for this three-dimensional context. We address several critical issues, including the necessity of projection, color assignment, forward iteration sets, and parameterization. By employing diverse visualization strategies, we successfully render the attractors and chaotic invariant sets of these maps. Furthermore, we illustrate applications involving the coexistence of multiple invariant sets, periodic bifurcations, and the local bifurcation of periodic tori. The work concludes by proposing potential future research directions. Our findings provide a foundation for developing efficient and accurate visualizing tools for this class of generalized systems. The paper is structured as follows: Section 2 defines the three-dimensional generalized Hénon map; Section 3 discusses foundational concepts and foundational visualization approaches; Section 4 addresses application-specific considerations and proposes two distinct visualization methods; Section 5 presents various application results; and Section 6 offers concluding remarks. This research aims to establish a framework for developing effective and precise visualization tools for three-dimensional generalized Hénon maps. Viewed as a specific instance of quadratic polynomial diffeomorphisms, the results of this paper can be extended to other quadratic polynomial diffeomorphisms in three dimensions. This work represents an extension of our prior publications \cite{Liu2019, Liu2019-2}. For additional visualizations of three-dimensional generalized Hénon maps, see \cite{Li2019}. To facilitate the presentation, we adopt the fo